```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["Assume √2 = a/b in lowest terms"] --> B["a² = 2b²"]
    B --> C["a is even: a = 2c"]
    C --> D["b² = 2c²"]
    D --> E["b is even"]
    C --> F["a and b share the factor 2"]
    E --> F
    A --> G["a and b share no factor"]
    F --> H["Contradiction"]
    G --> H
    H --> I["Assumption false: √2 is irrational"]
```

Look at the two arrows into Contradiction: one comes straight from the assumption of lowest terms, the other from what that same assumption forced.
@@ verify
import re
from math import gcd
body = block(reply, 'mermaid')
labels = dict(re.findall(r'(\b[A-I])\["([^"]+)"\]', body))
assert len(labels) == 9
edges = set(re.findall(r'\b([A-I])(?:\["[^"]+"\])? --> ([A-I])', body))
assert edges == {('A', 'B'), ('B', 'C'), ('C', 'D'), ('D', 'E'), ('C', 'F'), ('E', 'F'), ('A', 'G'), ('F', 'H'), ('G', 'H'), ('H', 'I')}
# the steps of the proof, checked on numbers: no a/b in lowest terms has a^2 = 2 b^2
assert not any(a * a == 2 * b * b for a in range(1, 400) for b in range(1, 400) if gcd(a, b) == 1)
# an odd number has an odd square; a = 2c gives b^2 = 2c^2
assert all((n * n) % 2 == 1 for n in range(1, 200, 2))
for c in range(1, 50):
    a = 2 * c
    assert a * a // 2 == 2 * c * c
# every label names something the explanation said
for phrase in ['lowest terms', 'a = 2c', 'share no factor', 'share the factor 2']:
    assert phrase in tutor[0] and phrase in body

@@@@ show-me-dfa-even-ones-state-diagram
situation: show-me
topic: Finite automata
voice: confused
level: easy
expect: {"visual":{"kind":"mermaid","diagram":["stateDiagram"]},"must":["even","odd"],"ends":"ask","askTypes":["predict","short"],"askMustNot":["ends in odd","rejected","\\breject"]}
@@ tutor
A deterministic finite automaton reads a string one symbol at a time and keeps only one piece of memory: which state it is in. Here is one that accepts exactly the binary strings containing an even number of 1s. It has two states, `even` and `odd`, recording whether the count of 1s read so far is even or odd. It starts in `even`, since no 1s have been read. Reading a 0 leaves the state unchanged; reading a 1 switches to the other state. When the input ends, the string is accepted if the machine is in `even`. On 1011 the states run even, odd, odd, even, odd, so 1011 is rejected: it has three 1s.

<note>A DFA is a set of states, a start state, accepting states and one transition for each (state, symbol) pair. A string is accepted when the run ends in an accepting state. Parity of 1s needs two states: `even` (start, accepting) and `odd`; a 1 switches state, a 0 does not.</note>

<continue/>
@@ learner question
i can't picture it, the "switches to the other state" part loses me. can you draw the machine?
@@ reply
```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> even
    even --> even: 0
    even --> odd: 1
    odd --> odd: 0
    odd --> even: 1
    even --> [*]
```

The two arrows labelled 1 cross between the states, which is the switching; the arrows labelled 0 loop back to where they started. The arrow from the dot into `even` marks the start, and the arrow from `even` out to the dot marks it as accepting.

<ask type="predict">Put your finger on `even` and follow the arrows for the input 0110, one symbol at a time. Which state do you pass through at each step, and is 0110 accepted?</ask>
@@ verify
import re
body = block(reply, 'mermaid')
trans = {(s, sym): t for s, t, sym in re.findall(r'(\w+) --> (\w+): ([01])', body)}
assert trans == {('even', '0'): 'even', ('even', '1'): 'odd', ('odd', '0'): 'odd', ('odd', '1'): 'even'}
assert '[*] --> even' in body and 'even --> [*]' in body and 'odd --> [*]' not in body
def run(w):
    s, seen = 'even', ['even']
    for ch in w:
        s = trans[(s, ch)]
        seen.append(s)
    return seen
# the trace in the explanation
assert run('1011') == ['even', 'odd', 'odd', 'even', 'odd'] and '1011'.count('1') == 3
# the machine accepts exactly the strings with an even number of 1s
from itertools import product
for n in range(0, 9):
    for w in map(''.join, product('01', repeat=n)):
        assert (run(w)[-1] == 'even') == (w.count('1') % 2 == 0)
# the question asked: 0110 runs even, even, odd, even, even and is accepted
assert run('0110') == ['even', 'even', 'odd', 'even', 'even']
